  "lineage": "Pernambuco (Recife-and-Olinda) Afro-Brazilian cortejo-procession carnival tradition of the nação-de-baque-virado coronation-pageant lineage 1700s-onward, where rei-and-rainha Black-royal-couple-and-court led by maracatu-baque-virado heavy-mid-tempo percussion processions enact symbolic Afro-Catholic-Yoruba-Bantu syncretic-king-coronation ceremony. The baque-virado rhythm features alfaia barrel-drums (two-headed rope-tensioned), caixa snare-with-bottom-strings, gonguê single-cowbell, and ganzá shaker, with calunga doll-and-staff iconography carried by the procession. The baque-solto rural-zonal variant adds woodwind-and-trumpet melodic-elements and faster-tempo agitation. The genre seeded 1990s mangue-beat fusion-rock and remains central to Recife carnival.",
